      "item_id": "ead4cdfb34d1b428c1cd760b",
      "version_id": "2440cc1580de805fb537ea10d57c87f518177ce7fa5c4cf63e2260a65098b0dc",
      "decision": "accept",
      "reason": "Accept as a qualified physical-lab contrast and practitioner account. Supplied publication metadata places the interview on October 8, within September 16–October 15; it does not date the underlying experiments. Concrete material includes a reported loading-error diagnosis (s00232, [51:40–52:51](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=3100,3171)) and preserving experimental lineage (s00267, [58:13–58:44](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=3493,3524)). These support student-facing inferences about logging provenance and checking anomalous results, not established gains in autonomous discovery. Preserve the explicit correction of the host's unambiguous-measurement premise: the guests describe ambiguity, replicates and complementary measurements (s00072–s00082, [22:57–24:46](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=1377,1486)). Their account also identifies full autonomy as a non-goal (s00230, [50:46–51:39](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=3046,3099)). Novelty is the recent operational account, not a demonstrated new breakthrough. The supplied interview provides no inspectable benchmark, matched baseline, held-out evaluation, quantified budget, ablation or independent replication for these claims. Founder commercial interests are explicit in the discussion of selling their tools to industry (s00341, [75:06–76:53](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=4506,4613)); another founder interview would not independently corroborate them. Independent researcher assessment remains missing.",
      "piece_ids": ["02", "03"]
    },
    {
      "item_id": "youtube-R9momwXV9w4",
      "version_id": "81ddd8b2230f42d36bc234d2235a06343303c4274511e44bb5cd8b1409bafab7",
      "decision": "defer",
      "reason": "September 24 publication is within the supplied window, but the relevant new research-evaluation claims lack supplied original supporting evidence. The narrator reports approximately 56% on an internal diagnostic benchmark and an 85% substitution threshold (s00075–s00082, [7:06–7:52](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=426)). The system card, task definitions, held-out safeguards, budgets, hardware and reproducible results are absent, so those figures cannot establish autonomous research competence. Preserve rather than average the apparent tension between a reported absence of sustained twofold acceleration (s00072–s00074, [6:48–7:07](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=408)) and a reported external evaluator's probabilistic assessment (s00093–s00095, [8:47–9:03](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=527)); these could concern different measurements or uncertainty, which the supplied material cannot resolve. The 2024 commitments and July 2026 revisions discussed at s00085–s00099 are older events, not September developments. Relevant leads concern evaluation validity, but the broader forecasts do not supply an inspectable student experiment. Commercially interested lab claims remain mediated through commentary; the narrator also promotes his own benchmark (s00254, [22:54–23:00](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=1374)), an ownership interest rather than independent validation. No additional financial relationship is established. Reconsider when original evaluation reports and full researcher recordings are supplied.",
      "piece_ids": []
    },
    {
      "item_id": "youtube-wMQB33rM28I",
      "version_id": "91b5117a082251bc859d7cb81ba933c439e1b0450075c2bf98b2b8112bb7d58e",
      "decision": "accept",
      "reason": "Accept the October 11 tutorial as an operational procedure for a small computational experiment, not evidence of measured improvement. Its useful contribution is a concrete workflow adaptation; the original autoresearch project is background, with no new release date established here. Despite the title, the speaker explicitly keeps the underlying model unchanged and varies instructions (s00010–s00013, [0:51–1:10](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=51)). The procedure separates editable instructions from fixed grading, checks the evaluator and reserves unseen tasks (s00050–s00074, [4:02–5:57](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=242)); it also explains held-out contamination and limited generalization (s00136–s00157, [10:27–12:07](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=627)). Budget planning explicitly counts 48 proposed worker trials plus additional overhead and calls for external stopping controls (s00160–s00172, [12:17–13:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=737)). These are useful design lessons, not observed costs or results: inspectable execution logs, actual instruction diffs, outcome tables and independent replication are not supplied. Defer the isolated automatic-transcript wording 'Keep rerunning one version until it gets lucky' pending original-audio verification (s00105, [8:09–8:14](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=489)); it conflicts with adjacent fixed-attempt guidance at s00103–s00106 and must not be attributed as deliberate advice. Channel membership and donation promotion establish a commercial audience interest (s00194–s00195, [14:49–15:00](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=889)); no vendor sponsorship is established. Under policy v2, missing results restrict the evidence role rather than disqualifying this useful tutorial.",
      "piece_ids": ["02", "03"]
    }
  ]
}
